        System.Diagnostics.Debugger.IsAttached = true;
#endif
```
Be familiar with the debugging techniques and tools like debugger, Visual Studio, breakpoints, single stepping, etc. to use these asserts properly.--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(OLD(_size) + 1 == RET); // ArrayList.Add(value)
Debug.Assert(OLD(_size) + c.Count == RET); // ArrayList.AddRange(c)
Debug.Assert(RET.Count == 0); // ArrayList.Clear()
Debug.Assert(OLD(RET) == value); // ArrayList.Contains(value)
Debug.Assert(OLD(_size) - 1 == RET); // ArrayList.RemoveAt(index)
Debug.Assert(0 <= index && index <= _size); // OLD(ArrayList.IndexOf(value))
```
Please note that for C# code assertions `OLD(variable_name)` refers to value of variable before operation `RET` refers to value after the operation. Also, the function should be declared correctly in C#. But these are assertions so they can not directly be written in code as it will result in syntax error. These need to be run by your IDE or your application code directly.--------------------Please find the solution below:

